Molecular Medicine, Peter Gilgan Centre for Research and Learning, The Hospital for Sick Children, 686 Bay Street, Toronto, ON M5G 0A4, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Roberts CES","lastName":"Roberts","firstName":"Charles E S","initials":"CES","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Relation Therapeutics, London, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Bronstein MM","lastName":"Bronstein","firstName":"Michael M","initials":"MM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Relation Therapeutics, London, UK; Department of Computer Science, University of Oxford, Oxford, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Lairson LL","lastName":"Lairson","firstName":"Luke L","initials":"LL","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Chemistry, The Scripps Research Institute, La Jolla, CA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Taylor-King JP","lastName":"Taylor-King","firstName":"Jake P","initials":"JP","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Relation Therapeutics, London, UK. Electronic address: jake@relationrx.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Bengio Y","lastName":"Bengio","firstName":"Yoshua","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Mila, the Quebec AI Institute, Montreal, QC, Canada."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5786-0798"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1112-0994"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0049-2044"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"10","volume":"3","journalIssueId":3653721,"dateOfPublication":"2023 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2023,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Cell Rep Methods","title":"Cell reports methods","essn":"2667-2375","issn":"2667-2375","nlmid":"9918227360606676","isoabbreviation":"Cell Rep Methods"},"printPublicationDate":"2023-10-01"},"pubYear":"2023","pageInfo":"100599","abstractText":"For large libraries of small molecules, exhaustive combinatorial chemical screens become infeasible to perform when considering a range of disease models, assay conditions, and dose ranges. Deep learning models have achieved state-of-the-art results in silico for the prediction of synergy scores. However, databases of drug combinations are biased toward synergistic agents and results do not generalize out of distribution. During 5 rounds of experimentation, we employ sequential model optimization with a deep learning model to select drug combinations increasingly enriched for synergism and active against a cancer cell line-evaluating only ∼5% of the total search space. Moreover, we find that learned drug embeddings (using structural information) begin to reflect biological mechanisms. In silico benchmarking suggests search queries are ∼5-10× enriched for highly synergistic drug combinations by using sequential rounds of evaluation when compared with random selection or ∼3× when using a pretrained model.","affiliation":"Mila, the Quebec AI Institute, Montreal, QC, Canada.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["brief-report","Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation","grantId":"INV-019229","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Engineering and Physical Sciences Research Council","grantId":"EP/X040062/1","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Neoplasms","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DT","qualifierName":"drug therapy"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Drug Combinations"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Computational Biology","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Drug Synergism"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Oncology",